# Favlo web completion

Completing a feature includes its happy path in the same change. A feature is anything a person can start from the app, including a new action on a screen that already has a script, and including a `web-api/` change the app can trigger. The change adds `web-spa/e2e/<feature>.spec.ts` or extends the script that owns that action. The script performs the action and shows its outcome. A script that does not perform the action does not count, including a row in the table below. Add a row when the script is new.

Write that script before treating the feature as finished. Then run it. The feature is finished when that command passes. An instruction to leave the suite unrun still leaves the script in the change.

A change a person cannot start, and that leaves the screen the same, adds neither check below.

## Native MAF and AG-UI

Use the Microsoft Agent Framework and AG-UI implementations that already perform the step. A tool call, a task result, a stream event, and a run lifecycle stay on those paths.

A workaround is host code that replaces or short-circuits that behavior. It includes returning early from a tool, polling a protocol the client already resolves, inventing a follow-up turn to deliver a result, or passing an escape hatch such as `allow_claimed=True`.

Do not add a workaround until the operator approves that workaround. Name the native call and why it cannot be used. Approval of one workaround does not cover the next.

For MCP tasks, the native path is a caller-owned `mcp.Client` with `TasksClientExtension`. `Client.call_tool` resolves the task to a tool result before the function returns, and AG-UI emits that result in the same run.

## Stable prefix cache

Any algorithm or implementation that builds the model prompt for a chat or workspace thread must keep a stable token prefix until the person clears that chat.

The AG-UI thread snapshot is the conversation record for that thread. Later turns append. They do not rewrite earlier rows, drop server-owned history, or rebuild the same facts in a different shape.

Rules:

- Leave prior messages byte-stable once saved. Do not edit, reorder, or strip them on a later turn.
- Put model-only injected context (selection cues, reminders, host notes) on a durable fenced `user` host cue (`<system_reminder>…</system_reminder>`), not spliced into the person's typed body. Mark it with AG-UI `metadata.favlo.ui=hidden`. Persist that row in the thread snapshot so the next turn replays it unchanged.
- Do not use mid-chat `system` or `developer` for those cues.
- Do not inject context only for the current request and omit it from history. That makes the next prompt diverge from the cached prefix.
- Hide host cues (and other non-transcript rows) in the transcript projection. Hiding from the UI is not a reason to drop them from the snapshot.
- The trusted server writes host-cue rows into the durable snapshot before the run. Clients must not supply them.
- Silent server cues (the gallery greeting) use that same fenced host-cue channel (plus an empty user pad the transcript omits). Do not delete snapshot rows after the run to hide them.
- Tool schemas that the model has already used in a thread must stay available on later turns until clear. Persist AG-UI progressive MCP loads (`persist_loaded` / `restore_loaded`). Prefer eager gallery tools over a mid-turn load step that changes the schema.
- Clear and archive mint a new thread. Only then may the prefix start over.
- A chat turn sets `store` true. The thread snapshot keeps the latest response id. The next model call sends that id as `previous_response_id` and only the new turn. The listener prepends the stored chain, including reasoning, before the pipeline renders the prompt. A missing id starts a new chain.

A design that needs a fact every turn without growing history must use a channel that is identical whenever that fact is unchanged (for example frozen instructions that do not include turn-varying text). A turn-varying fact belongs in durable history when the model must remember it.

## Borders and splitters

Painted border width is not the hit target. Keep seams quiet; make interaction targets comfortable.

| Role | Visible width | Hit target | Paint |
|---|---|---|---|
| Passive panel / column seam | `1` | n/a | `palette.border` or `palette.borderStrong` |
| Resizable splitter (wide web) | `1` at rest; `2` on hover, focus, or drag | at least `space[3]` (12) centered on the seam | hairline + optional centered grip pill; track stays transparent until hot, then `palette.accentSubtle` |
| Control / input outline | `1` | the control | `palette.border` or `palette.borderStrong`; focus uses `palette.focusRing` (see `Pressable`) |

Do not fill the whole grab strip as a solid border bar. A fat painted divider (for example an 8px `backgroundColor` track with no hairline) reads as chrome, not as a seam.

For a resizable edge:

- Hairline uses `palette.borderStrong` at rest and `palette.accent` when hot.
- A short grip pill on the seam (about 4×28, `radius.pill`) marks that the edge moves; omit it only when the boundary is not meant to invite drag.
- Cursor is `col-resize` or `row-resize`. Keyboard arrows (and Home/End when useful) change the size. Double-click may restore the default size.
- Gallery wide chat uses `web-spa/src/workspace/GalleryChatResizeHandle.tsx` as the reference.

Token colors only. Pair with [web-spa.mdc](web-spa.mdc) for surfaces and contrast.

## Curated gallery and workspace shell

The gallery workspace is generative UI on a shared shell (closed catalog, agent placement, AG-UI state). The single as-built note is in the closed tree: [favlo-cloud/docs/maf/curated-gallery.md](../../../favlo-cloud/docs/maf/curated-gallery.md). When a change touches that surface, update that note in the same change. Keep short pointers only in [web-spa/README.md](../../web-spa/README.md), [web-api/README.md](../../web-api/README.md), and [docs/ai-assistance/README.md](../../docs/ai-assistance/README.md).

Catalog names and versions in `web_api/catalog.py` and `web-spa/src/workspace/catalog.ts` stay paired. A new element updates both registries, the guide when it may place it, the as-built note, and the happy-path script for that action.

## Unit tests

Use Jest with the `jest-expo` preset and `@testing-library/react-native`. Router tests use `expo-router/testing-library` and live outside `app/`. These tests do not start `favlo serve` or a browser.

```bash
cd web-spa && npx jest <test>
```

## End-to-end

Playwright simulates the person in headless Chromium. The script is deterministic: one fixed sequence of clicks, typing, and keys. It does not branch on model wording, timings, or the text of a reply. `favlo web` is running, and it calls a live `favlo serve`. Do not stub the model or a media tool.

Never use the Cursor IDE browser, Computer Use, or any interactive browser MCP to test or drive the Favlo web app. Those tools are not deterministic, can open native dialogs, and are not the project’s evidence. Always use the headless Playwright scripts under `web-spa/e2e/` (and Jest for unit tests). Stub only what the script already stubs (for example `**/folders/pick`); do not drive the macOS folder dialog by hand.

```bash
cd web-spa && npx playwright test e2e/<feature>.spec.ts --project=chromium
```

The list reporter prints each test and each `step(...)` as it starts. Prefer `step` from `e2e/progress.ts` for long agent waits. Do not pipe the run through `tail`; that hides live progress.

The script is `web-spa/e2e/<feature>.spec.ts`. A fresh install uses `e2e/web-new-user-e2e-setup/`. `web-spa/playwright.config.ts` sets `screenshot` to `on`.

